  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front is currently tracking through the region early this\nmorning. In the vicinity of the front there are light rain\nshowers between Pittsburgh and the Laurel Highlands. Breezy \nconditions are also being observed with the front, but gusts are\nonly peaking between 20-25mph. Rain chances will decrease after\n11am this morning with incoming dry air. \n\nChances of lake-enhanced showers increase north of I-80 after \nsunset which may transition to lake-enhanced snow showers \novernight. Any accumulations on pavement/asphalt will be \nchallenged given recent warm temperatures, but some light \naccumulations on grassy/elevated surfaces are possible. High- \nend possibilities for accumulations only total ~1\" for northern \nMercer/Venango County, but most RRFS members show a light \naccumulation favored, primarily north of I-80.\n\nMostly clear skies are favored Tuesday night, which will\ncertainty bring the coolest temperatures of the week, and\npossibly some of the coldest temperatures of this season thus \nfar. Widespread 20s are forecast for lows, with some isolated \nteens possible in small, insulated drainage basins.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThere is higher confidence in the forecast for Thanksgiving and\nBlack Friday, with an increasing amount of ensemble members \nconverging on a pattern whereby a closing upper Plains low \nmerges with a developing southeast low Wednesday through early \nThursday. At the same time, a coastal low is expected to develop\noff the southeast coast, and both lows are expected to move \nnorth in tandem. \n\nThere are still a couple scenarios with differences in QPF. \nThere first one would be a coastal low occlusion earlier, with \nthe stacked low tracking across central Pennsylvania, putting \nthe region in a deformation zone with broad ascent in the right \nentrance region of a cyclonically curved jet. This would result \nin higher QPF, with high end totals showing localized amounts of\n2-2.5\" are possible (most representative to NBM 90th \npercentile). This may also bring in a stronger backside pressure\ngradient with cooling Friday which might result in some snow in\nhigher terrain Friday into Saturday with wind gusts approaching\nadvisory criteria. In the other scenario, a generally slower \nocclusion with a dominant coastal low upon passage would lead to\na faster progression, more representative of localized totals \nup to 0.75-1.25\", which is still quite a soaker. The backside \ngradient winds may be slightly weather and snow chances for high\nterrain slightly less likely. \n\nIn any event, Thanksgiving seems wet, with most ensembles \nshowing the highest rain rates around dinner time. Black Friday\nmay be a bit more showery (with some chance of snow\naccumulations for the high terrain), but winds could be gusty \nfor much of the day. The rest of the holiday weekend seems\ncomparatively quieter.",
